NER errors are dominated by missed entities (43--65\% of errors), which correlate with count rather than confusability; pairwise SCS captures individual type confusion but this signal is diluted at the batch level by the overwhelming contribution of boundary detection failures. BCS is thus a useful pre-hoc screening tool for RE schema design but not for NER, where count reduction is the primary lever.

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The bounded count$\times$confusability interaction (0.47\,pp, TOST $p = 0.003$) means practitioners need not worry about confusability amplification at scale. For RE, type pairs in the upper SCS quartile are associated with 3--5\,pp accuracy differences at batch level. Few-shot demonstrations do not mitigate confusability: the BCS penalty widens under 5-shot prompting (+4.3\,pp, $p = 0.014$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_fewshot_bcs}). These results apply to the training-free setting; fine-tuned models may learn type boundaries that reduce confusability effects.

